From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tiberiu Chibici <chibicitiberiu@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 09:17:48 +0300
Subject: mixer: no integer-to-float round trips through memory on the PPE

The PS3's PPE (Cell) has no move between its integer, floating-point and vector registers: a
conversion stores the value and loads it back, and the load waits for the store to drain
(load-hit-store, about 40 cycles). The mixer did that once per sample of every voice twice over
(the resampler's fraction, the 16-bit source sample) and once per output sample (the 16-bit
output), which a qemu count put at about half of the mixer's estimated PPE cycles.

- Resample_Linear/Cubic: the fraction's float from two 256-entry tables (exact, the same value).
- LoadSamples<i16>: AltiVec, eight samples at a time (vec_ctf(x, 15) is x/32768 exactly), for
  mono and interleaved stereo buffers; the rest as before.
- Write<i16>, stereo: AltiVec vec_cts/vec_packs/vec_merge, SampleConv<i16>'s clamp and truncation.

The tables apply on any 64-bit PowerPC before POWER8; the AltiVec parts need -maltivec (our
-mcpu=cell). Checked under qemu against the unpatched source with the same flags, 16 voices
through a loopback device: the mixer's estimated PPE cycles fall 55% (instructions 17%), and
the output is bit-identical (mono and stereo sources, 22.05 and 44.1 kHz, float and 16-bit
output, spline and linear resampling).

